This self-consciousness paralyzed them. Their heartbeats stuttered out of sync. Weather patterns grew chaotic. The Sky Weaver felt their anxiety spiral.

She tried reassuring them, but they couldn't hear through their doubt. So she tried something radical: she withdrew her presence completely for one full day.

The descendants panicked initially. Then something unexpected happened. Without the Sky Weaver to perform for - or even to be conscious for - they simply existed. A child laughed at rain without thinking about connection. An elder cried genuine tears. Someone danced without checking if they were dancing "authentically."

When the Sky Weaver returned, they understood. Authenticity isn't achieved by monitoring yourself. It emerges when you stop asking if you're being authentic and simply engage with what's actually happening.

"The question 'Am I really present?'" the old woman noted, "prevents you from being present."

The Sky Weaver wove that paradox into clouds that only appear when you're not looking for them.

